### Weekly Report Generator -- API request collection
### JetBrains HTTP Client format (IntelliJ IDEA / WebStorm "Run" gutter icons, or the `ijhttp` CLI).
### Also readable as plain request text by VS Code's REST Client extension, EXCEPT the response
### handler scripts ("> {% ... %}") used below to capture the XSRF-TOKEN cookie -- those are a
### JetBrains-only feature. Run this file's requests top to bottom within one environment; IntelliJ's
### HTTP Client keeps a cookie jar per environment automatically, so access_token/refresh_token set
### by Login are sent on every later request with no extra work. The one thing that ISN'T automatic
### is the X-XSRF-TOKEN header CSRF requires on state-changing calls -- that's what the response
### handler after "Get CSRF Token" captures into {{xsrf}}.
###
### Select the "dev" environment (http-client.env.json) before running anything here.


### =====================================================================================
### 1. Authentication
### =====================================================================================

### Login as admin -- sets access_token + refresh_token httpOnly cookies. No token in the body.
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "admin@example.com",
  "password": "Password123"
}


### Get the CSRF cookie + capture it into {{xsrf}} for every state-changing request below.
# @name csrf
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/csrf

> {%
    const setCookie = response.headers.valueOf("Set-Cookie") || "";
    const match = setCookie.match(/XSRF-TOKEN=([^;]+)/);
    if (match) {
        client.global.set("xsrf", match[1]);
    }
%}


### Current authenticated user (reads the access_token cookie).
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/me


### An Authorization: Bearer header is ignored -- there is no header-based auth path any more.
### Expect 401 even though this is a real, currently-valid JWT.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer not-read-by-anything


### Rotate the refresh token and get a fresh access token. Requires X-XSRF-TOKEN (CSRF-protected
### even though it's in Spring Security's permitAll() list -- see docs/api-design.md Section 2).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/refresh
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}


### Log out -- revokes the current refresh token and clears both cookies.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}


### Request a password reset email. Always 202 with the same body, known email or not.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/forgot-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "member@example.com"
}


### Reset with a token from the emailed link (Mailpit at http://localhost:8025 in dev, or read
### straight from the `password_reset_tokens` table's hash -- the raw token is never stored, so it
### can only come from the actual email/link).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/reset-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "token": "paste-the-raw-token-from-the-emailed-link",
  "newPassword": "NewPassword2"
}


### =====================================================================================
### 2. Invitations
### =====================================================================================

### Create an invitation (admin only). 409 if an active account or pending invite already exists.
# @name createInvitation
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/invitations
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
  "email": "newhire@example.com",
  "role": "TEAM_MEMBER"
}


### List invitations (admin only), optionally filtered by status.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/invitations?status=PENDING&page=0&size=10


### Resend -- rotates the token, invalidating the previous link.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/invitations/1/resend
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}


### Revoke -- the token becomes permanently unusable.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/invitations/1
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}


### Validate an invitation token (anonymous, CSRF-exempt) -- what the accept-invitation page calls
### first to show who's being invited.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/invitations/validate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "token": "paste-the-raw-token-from-the-emailed-link"
}


### Accept an invitation (anonymous, CSRF-exempt) -- creates the account with the role fixed by the
### invitation itself.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/invitations/accept
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "token": "paste-the-raw-token-from-the-emailed-link",
  "name": "New Hire",
  "password": "Password123"
}


### =====================================================================================
### 3. Admin -- User Management
### =====================================================================================

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/users?page=0&size=10

###
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/users/1

###
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/users/1
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
  "name": "Updated Name",
  "email": "updated@example.com"
}

### Change role.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/users/1/role
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
  "role": "MANAGER"
}

### Deactivate (soft delete) -- also revokes every refresh token this user is holding.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/users/1
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

### Reactivate.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/users/1/activate
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

### Trigger a password-reset email for this user (admin never sets/sees the new password).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/users/1/send-password-reset
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}


